# Text is stored the way the container reads it, whichever operating system did
# the editing.
#
# A clone whose bytes depend on the platform that made it is a repository where
# one commit has two different checksums, where somebody's diff is mostly
# invisible carriage returns, and where the specific case at the bottom of this
# file has to be remembered for the next script anyone adds. Pinning the whole
# tree is what stops that rule being a rumour.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The two test chimes are bytes, not text. The heuristic above is allowed to be
# wrong about a file nobody reads, and normalising one of these quietly changes
# the fixture several playback tests measure against.

*.wav binary

# The one file in this repository that a container executes.

# A shell script with Windows line endings has a shebang that reads `#!/bin/sh\r`,
# which names an interpreter that does not exist, and the container dies with
# "no such file or directory" before the program ever runs. That is what
# `core.autocrlf=true` - the usual setting on Windows - hands to a clone of this
# repository, and docs/HOW-IT-WORKS.md tells Windows users to build the image
# themselves, so they would meet that error with nothing wrong in their command.
# Pinned here rather than left to a setting nobody remembers changing.
*.sh text eol=lf
